/* 
========================================================
          ADDING CUSTOM FONTS — GOOGLE FONTS
========================================================

Google Fonts provides free web fonts you can use in websites.
There are 3 main ways to add them:

1. `<link>` tag in HTML `<head>` (recommended)  
2. `@import` inside a CSS file  
3. Self-hosting via `@font-face` (advanced / for full control)

Each method loads the font files from Google's servers; you must also use proper CSS `font-family` rules and fallbacks.

--------------------------------------------------------
1) METHOD 1 — <link> TAG IN HTML (RECOMMENDED)
--------------------------------------------------------
- Fastest and best-performing method.
- Add the <link> inside the <head> section of HTML.

Example:
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins:wght@300;400;600&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">

Apply in CSS:
    body {
        font-family: "Poppins", Arial, sans-serif;
    }

Why use this method:
- Better performance than @import
- Loads fonts earlier
- Works well with browser caching
- Easy & recommended by Google

Tips:
- Use "preconnect" for performance boost.
- Use display=swap to avoid invisible text issues.
- Only request the weights you need.

--------------------------------------------------------
2) METHOD 2 — @import INSIDE CSS
--------------------------------------------------------
How: at top of your CSS file, put Google's @import URL.

- Add this at the TOP of your CSS file.

Example:
    @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Poppins:wght@300;400;600&display=swap');

    body {
        font-family: "Poppins", sans-serif;
    }

Notes:
- Keeps HTML clean.
- But slower than <link> because browser loads CSS first → then the import.
- Not recommended for performance-heavy sites.

--------------------------------------------------------
3) METHOD 3 — SELF-HOSTING WITH @font-face (ADVANCED)
--------------------------------------------------------
- Download .woff2 font files and store them in your project (e.g., /fonts/).
- Gives full control over speed, caching, privacy.

Example:
    @font-face {
        font-family: "MyPoppins";
        src: url("/fonts/Poppins-Regular.woff2") format("woff2");
        font-weight: 400;
        font-style: normal;
        font-display: swap;
    }

    body {
        font-family: "MyPoppins", sans-serif;
    }

Why self-host?
- Full control over speed & cache
- Works offline
- No external dependency

Downsides:
- You must manage font files
- More setup work

--------------------------------------------------------
GOOGLE’S CSS HELPER CLASSES (IMPORTANT NOTE)
--------------------------------------------------------
Google sometimes shows:

    .emilys-candy-regular {
        font-family: "Emilys Candy", serif;
        font-weight: 400;
        font-style: normal;
    }

This DOES NOT load the font.
You MUST still use <link>, @import, or @font-face.

--------------------------------------------------------
FALLBACK FONTS (IMPORTANT)
--------------------------------------------------------
Always give multiple fonts:

Example:
    body {
        font-family: "Poppins", "Helvetica Neue", Arial, sans-serif;
    }

Meaning:
- Use Poppins first.
- If missing → use Helvetica Neue.
- If missing → use Arial.
- If all fail → use generic sans-serif.

Why fallbacks?
- Prevents weird default fonts
- Ensures readability
- Required for good UI design

--------------------------------------------------------
GENERIC FONT FAMILIES (ALWAYS KEEP LAST)
--------------------------------------------------------
Generic families are basic built-in browser fonts:

- serif        → Times New Roman style
- sans-serif   → Arial style
- monospace    → all letters same width (Courier)
- cursive      → handwriting style
- fantasy      → decorative fonts

Used because:
- They ALWAYS exist on every device.
- They guarantee that text will display even if everything else fails.

--------------------------------------------------------
PERFORMANCE BEST PRACTICES
--------------------------------------------------------
✓ Prefer <link> over @import  
✓ Use preconnect for faster loading  
✓ Use display=swap  
✓ Only load required weights  
✓ Use woff2 when self-hosting  

========================================================
                 END OF NOTES
========================================================
*/
